      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives generally argue that legalizing physician-assisted suicide violates the sanctity of human life and usurps the exclusive role of God in determining when to end suffering. In contrast, religious liberals often contend that such laws better reflect a conscience-based approach to end-of-life care, focusing on compassion and alleviating the horror of unbearable suffering without necessarily denying individual autonomy. Both sides typically reference sacred texts to support their views, though they differ on whether the relevant moral principle prioritizes absolute preservation of life or the redemptive value of compassionate suffering.",
